{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "Do the links between substance use and friendship documented in racially mixed schools also appear in American Indian (AI)-majority reservation schools, where kinship, community, and identity may shape relationships differently?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "It\nremains unclear whether these patterns hold in settings where the student population\nis predominantly AI and where cultural norms surrounding kinship, community, and\nidentity may differently orient relational processes.",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "Consequently, we expect that substance use will show weaker sender,\nreceiver, and homophily effects in AI-majority schools than observed in heterogeneous populations.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "How do reciprocity, triadic closure, and the distribution of nominations organize these networks, and do the degree patterns suggest popularity hierarchies or constraints on them?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Our analysis focused on endogenous structural factors (reciprocity, transitivity, two-path effects, and degree distributions)",
            "page": 14
          },
          {
            "text": "We therefore explore the nature of degree effects, whether they indicate preferential\nattachment or egalitarian constraints, within these specific AI adolescent contexts.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Is depression associated with sending fewer nominations, receiving fewer nominations, or forming friendships with peers who have similar symptoms?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We\ntherefore examine which of these mechanisms—receiver effects, withdrawal-related sender effects, or homophily—\noperate in AI-majority school contexts.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Does the strength of AI identity affirmation matter for sending or receiving nominations or for choosing similar friends? The authors investigate this without a directional hypothesis.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "A theoretically important yet understudied attribute is the strength of adolescents’ identification with their AI\nheritage.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "Given the exploratory nature of this investigation, we\ndo not formulate directional hypotheses but examine sender, receiver, and homophily effects empirically.",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Do established patterns of same-gender and same-grade friendship recur in these schools, and does shared sexual orientation also pattern ties?",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Substantively, gender and grade homophily are among the most robust findings in adolescent network research [9, 12]; we expect\nthese patterns to replicate in AI-majority schools, where gender- and grade-segregated activities similarly organize peer\ninteraction.",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "Sexual orientation homophily has received limited attention in the literature, but documented social stratification along sexual identity lines in adolescent populations suggests\nthat it may likewise structure friendship ties [9].",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "In the three AI-majority reservation schools studied, mutual ties, friends-of-friends ties, and shared demographic characteristics organized friendship networks more consistently than substance use, depression, or AI identity affirmation did.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Results indicated that universal structural mechanisms\n(reciprocity, transitivity) and demographic homophily (gender, grade, sexual orientation) were primary drivers of friendship.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "However, unlike what is often observed in\nstudies of other adolescent populations, substance use showed no consistent association with sending ties or receiving ties. Similarly, depression and AI identity were not\nsubstantively associated with friendship formation.",
            "page": 1
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors argue that network-based prevention should not presume that substance use organizes friendships. They call for approaches tailored to each school's relationships and grounded in strengths that already connect AI youth.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This heterogeneity carries practical implications: network-based interventions for substance use prevention may need to be calibrated to\nschool-specific relational ecologies rather than applied uniformly across reservation settings.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "Gaining insight into these alternative predictors is\ncrucial for developing innovative, effective strategies for substance use prevention tailored to AI youth that may center on\nstrengths-based connections rather than social ties rooted in risky behavior.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors conducted a cross-sectional survey of ninth- and tenth-graders in three purposively selected schools on one Northern Plains reservation. Of 263 survey respondents, 247 had complete network data: 49 in the small tribal school, 128 in the larger tribal school, and 70 in the private college-preparatory school.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "In our study, we established partnerships with three schools on a large Northern Plains reservation to conduct a comprehensive cross-sectional social network survey among 9th- and 10th-grade students. The schools were purposefully\nselected to represent a broad spectrum of social environments on the reservation.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "We\nhad an overall response rate of 77% across the three schools. Specifically, 72% of eligible students completed surveys in\nSchool A (n = 52 out of 72), 76% in School B (n = 140 out of 184), and 84% in School C (n = 71 out of 84), for a total sample\nsize of 263.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Of the 247 participants with complete network data, item non-response was limited to the substance use variables.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "School A (n = 49) was the smallest, located in\na geographically isolated village; School B (n = 128) was the largest, serving a more populous community; and School C\n(n = 70) was a private college-preparatory institution.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "Our selection included a tribal school\nin one of the most geographically isolated and smallest villages (school A), another larger tribal school in a more populous\nvillage (school B)",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The two-part survey combined individual measures with friendship nominations. The study used Network Canvas, and community and youth advisory boards reviewed the measures for cultural appropriateness.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The survey consisted of two primary sections: the first collected demographic information, measures of AI identity, depression, and substance use; the second collected directed friendship nominations.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "We used Network Canvas, a software\nplatform designed for collecting social network data [32]. All measures were reviewed for cultural appropriateness and\nrelevance by a Community Advisory Board and a Youth Advisory Board from the participating communities prior to finalization [33].",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Students could nominate up to ten close friends from grades 7–12 at their school, but the outcome analyzed was the directed network among responding ninth- and tenth-graders, with one adjacency matrix per school.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Students were\nasked to “select up to 10 friends you are closest with in grades 7th-12th at your school.” School rosters within the Network\nCanvas software assisted identification.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "For analysis, we focused on ties among responding 9th- and 10th-grade students,\nstored in a square adjacency matrix for each school.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Substance use was measured with separate yes-or-no self-reports of ever using alcohol, cigarettes, and marijuana; the cigarette question excluded ceremonial tobacco use.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Participants’ use of alcohol, tobacco, and marijuana was\nmeasured through binary variables (yes = 1; no = 0), derived from self-reported data in the survey’s initial section.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "“Have you ever had a drink (more than a sip) of alcohol, such as beer,\nwine, or hard liquor like vodka?”, “Have you ever smoked a cigarette? (Do not include ceremonial use of tobacco.)”, and\n“Have you ever used marijuana (also called weed, pot, chronic, or nug, including edibles)?”",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "For students who declined to answer a substance-use item, the authors coded use when more than half of the peers who nominated them reported that they used the substance at least a few times a year. Excluding these cases did not change the direction of any focal estimate or reverse a substantive conclusion.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "If over half of a respondent’s incoming friendship nominations (i.e., peers who had nominated them as a friend)\nindicated peer perception of them engaging in a specific substance use behavior (response ≥ 2, corresponding to ‘a few\ntimes a year’ or more), the respondent was classified as a user (1) for that variable",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "sensitivity\nanalyses that exclude these cases entirely leave the direction of every focal effect unchanged and reverse no substantive conclusion",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Depression was measured with six CES-D symptom questions that asked students to rate symptom frequency from 0 to 3.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The survey also included a measure of depression, utilizing the Center for Epidemiologic\nStudies Depression Scale (CES-D) [35–38]. This section of the survey consisted of six questions, each probing different\nfacets of depression, such as persistent sadness, feelings of failure, loneliness, and the occurrence of crying spells.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Respondents were asked to indicate the frequency of these symptoms using a scale ranging from 0 (rarely) to 3 (most or\nall of the time).",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "AI identity was measured as the mean of six ethnic-identity affirmation and belonging items whose wording was adapted with the advisory boards for the participating tribal community.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The measurement of AI identity was assessed using six items adapted from the Affirmation and Belonging\nsubscale of the Multigroup Ethnic Identity Measure (MEIM) [39].",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "For cultural relevance and specificity within this tribal community,\nitem wording was adapted in partnership with the study’s Community and Youth Advisory Boards, with generic references\nto “TRIBE” replaced by the specific tribal identifier.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "An\naverage score across these six items was computed to represent overall AI identity affirmation.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Grade came from school rosters, while gender and sexual orientation were self-reported and grouped for analysis. The demographic terms served both as benchmarks for friendship among similar students and as adjustments for the focal associations.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The information about each respondent’s grade level was obtained based on school rosters\nprovided by the participating schools.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Since respondents could have chosen more than one option\nfor the gender question, we processed gender information by male (if male is one of the selected options), female\n(if female is one of the selected options), and other (if male and female are not in the selected options).",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "Given\nthe low prevalence of responses outside of the straight category, and to simplify the model, we categorized sexual\norientation as straight and non-straight.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "the homophily terms capture substantively meaningful patterns of friendship formation along demographic lines, which we report and interpret as\nreplication benchmarks.",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "serve as adjustment variables that account for demographic sorting in friendship nominations, so that the focal attribute\neffects can be interpreted net of these compositional forces.",
            "page": 6
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The descriptive data show that marijuana was the most commonly reported substance across schools. The observed networks had low-to-moderate density, high reciprocity, and moderate clustering.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "marijuana use was most common (37–43%), followed by alcohol (14–24%) and tobacco (16–23%).",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "Network densities ranged from 0.026 (School\nB) to 0.083 (School C), reflecting the low-to-moderate tie density typical of friendship nomination networks where students select from a restricted roster. Reciprocity rates were high across all schools (93.3–97.7%), indicating that mutual\nfriendship nominations were the norm.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "Transitivity\nlevels ranged from 31.0% (School B) to 36.5% (School A), suggesting moderate clustering.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors fitted school-specific exponential random graph models (ERGMs) to account for dependence among ties. Structural terms represented reciprocity, transitivity, incoming and outgoing degree, and open two-step paths, so that closure could be distinguished from the presence of a two-step path.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We employed Exponential Random Graph Models (ERGMs) to model the probability of friendship tie formation within\neach school, accounting for the inherent interdependence of network observations [9,24].",
            "page": 8
          },
          {
            "text": "We included standard parameters to account for structural network dependence: reciprocity\n(mutual ties), transitivity (modeled via the geometrically weighted edgewise shared partner, GWESP, term), and the\ndistribution of incoming and outgoing ties (modeled via geometrically weighted indegree, GWIDeg, and outdegree,\nGWODeg, terms) [23,24].",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Following Snijders et al. [28], we also included a two‑path term to separate the tendency for\nopen two‑step sequences from triadic closure, thereby allowing the GWESP coefficient to more cleanly capture transitivity.",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Each school had a base model with network structure, demographics, depression, and AI identity, plus separate models adding the sender, receiver, and similarity terms for each substance.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "This included a base model containing structural terms (reciprocity, transitivity [GWESP], degree distributions [GWIDeg, GWODeg], and a two-path term) and core substantive variables\n(demographics, depression, AI identity).",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "Subsequently, we estimated separate models for each substance (alcohol,\ntobacco, marijuana) that added sender (nodeofactor), receiver (nodeifactor), and homophily (nodematch) effects for the\nspecific substance use variable.",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "To compare magnitudes across schools, the authors reported average marginal effects: average changes in predicted tie probability for a one-unit change in a predictor with other variables held constant.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "To address this and quantify the substantive importance of each factor, we computed Average Marginal Effects (AMEs) using the ergMargins package [44].",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "AMEs represent\nthe average change in the predicted probability of a tie formation associated with a one-unit change in a predictor variable, holding all other variables constant. This provides an interpretable, population-averaged effect size that is directly\ncomparable across the three school networks [43].",
            "page": 9
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The models were estimated by Markov chain Monte Carlo maximum likelihood in R. All twelve converged, showed no evidence of drift or degeneracy, and adequately reproduced observed network statistics in simulation checks.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "All ERGMs were estimated using Markov Chain Monte Carlo Maximum Likelihood\nEstimation (MCMLE) via the ergm package (version 4.12.0) in R [42].",
            "page": 9
          },
          {
            "text": "all 12 models converged successfully within 25 iterations. MCMC\nchain mixing was evaluated via trace plots for all 12 models (Figs S13–S24 in S2 File), which showed no evidence of\ntrend or drift.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "Model degeneracy was assessed by inspecting coefficient estimates for extreme values and MCMC samples\nfor non-finite statistics; no degeneracy was detected in any specification.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "Goodness-of-fit was assessed by comparing the observed network to 100 networks simulated from each fitted model\n(Figs S1–S12 in S2 File): on degree distributions (indegree and outdegree), edgewise and dyadwise shared partner distributions, geodesic distances, and the triad census.",
            "page": 12
          },
          {
            "text": "In all 12 models, the observed network statistics fell within the central\nrange of simulated distributions, indicating adequate model fit.",
            "page": 12
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "A check for overadjustment removed depression, AI identity, and demographics from the substance-use models. The simpler models still did not show a robust substance-use pattern.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "To assess whether the absence of substance‑use effects might be due to overcontrol (i.e., the inclusion of depression, AI identity, and demographic covariates), we estimated separate ERGMs containing only structural terms and the\nsubstance use variables (alcohol, tobacco, marijuana).",
            "page": 14
          },
          {
            "text": "substance use showed no robust or consistent association with sender, receiver, or\nhomophily effects across schools, while the structural processes (reciprocity, transitivity, degree effects) remained significant.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Reciprocity and closure of friends-of-friends ties were consistent across schools. A subset of students sent disproportionately many nominations in all three schools, but incoming nominations were significantly concentrated only in the private preparatory school.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Reciprocity was positive and highly significant in every model, indicating a robust tendency for mutual friendship nominations.\nSimilarly, the positive coefficients for closed triads (transitivity) and negative coefficients for two‑path confirmed that triadic\nclosure—the process by which friends of friends become friends—is a fundamental organizing principle within these AI\nadolescent networks.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "The negative and significant coefficients for geometrically\nweighted outdegree (GWODeg) across all schools indicate a centralized structure in outgoing ties, meaning a subset\nof adolescents initiated a disproportionately high number of friendship nominations.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "For incoming ties (indegree), a\nsignificant negative coefficient was observed only in School C, indicating a centralized popularity structure in that setting.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Same-gender and same-grade friendships were more likely in all three schools. Shared sexual orientation also patterned ties, although the strength and statistical support varied by school and orientation group.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Gender and grade homophily were consistently\nsignificant across all schools (AME = 0.008–0.058, all p < 0.001), indicating that shared gender identity and grade level are\nprimary axes of friendship formation, as extensively documented in the broader adolescent literature [9,12].",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "Sexual Orientation – Straight\n\n0.017 (0.007)**\n\n0.003 (0.001)*\n\n0.006 (0.004)†\n\nSexual Orientation – Other\n\n0.028 (0.007)***\n\n0.002 (0.002)\n\n0.024 (0.005)***",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Substance use did not consistently predict who sent or received nominations or who befriended similar peers. The isolated signals were more nominations sent by alcohol users in School C, alcohol similarity in School B, and weaker evidence of marijuana similarity in School B; there was no tobacco similarity pattern.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Sender and\nreceiver effects—indicating whether individuals who use substances are more likely to receive or initiate friendships—\nwere almost entirely non‑significant across all three schools and substances.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "The only exception was a positive sender\neffect for alcohol use in School C (AME = 0.015, p < 0.05), suggesting a tendency for alcohol‑using adolescents to initiate\nmore friendship nominations in that setting.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "Alcohol use showed significant homophily in School B (AME = 0.0039, p < 0.01) but not in the other schools or for other\nsubstances. Marijuana use exhibited homophily in School B (AME = 0.0020, p < 0.10), while tobacco use showed no\nhomophily in any school.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Depression was not meaningfully associated with sending or receiving friendship nominations. Similarity in depression scores was detectable in two schools but too small to be substantively meaningful.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Depression sender and receiver effects were substantively negligible across all schools; although the sender effect in\nSchool B reached statistical significance across model specifications owing to very small standard errors, its magnitude\n(AME < 0.001) indicates no meaningful association",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "depression homophily was negligible in magnitude (statistically detectable only in Schools B and C;\nAME ≤ 0.001).",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "AI identity affirmation did not meaningfully distinguish who sent nominations or who befriended similar peers. There was only borderline evidence that students with stronger affirmation received more nominations in School C.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "AI identity was largely unassociated with sending or receiving ties,\nwith only a borderline positive receiver effect in School C.",
            "page": 13
          },
          {
            "text": "AI identity homophily was consistently non-significant across all schools.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The schools represent one reservation, not AI adolescents generally. The findings may not extend to racially mixed schools, and the authors could not obtain a suitable comparison sample of non-AI schools.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "First, we recognize that the results presented here represent\nonly three schools on one reservation. The history, policies, geography, and culture of this reservation all intersect with\nnetwork formation and maintenance patterns. Our findings may not extend to AI youth in racially heterogeneous schools,\nwhere Rees, Freng, and Winfree [17] observed different network characteristics.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "While a comparison sample of non‑AI schools\nwould strengthen generalizability, identifying a suitable dataset with comparable schools (similar size, rural location, and\ncontemporary data) was not feasible.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The survey captures one developmental period at one time point. It cannot show how friendships or behaviors change with age or through school transitions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Furthermore, we acknowledge that these data reflect only one\ndevelopmental epoch of adolescents at one time point.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "The literature highlights the role that school transitions (elementary to middle to high school) play in adolescent\nnetworks, but our cross-sectional design imposes a fundamental interpretive limit: a single, simultaneous observation\nof the network and of individual attributes cannot capture changes in network formation and behavior over time.",
            "page": 16
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Cross-sectional ERGMs cannot separate choosing similar friends from becoming more similar to friends. The focal estimates therefore do not establish causation in either direction.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "cross-sectional ERGMs cannot analytically distinguish selection processes—adolescents choosing friends\nwith similar attributes—from influence processes—friends becoming more similar to one another over time [4,18,48].",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "not as evidence that substance use, depression, or AI identity causally affect friendship formation, nor as\nevidence that friendship networks causally shape these attributes.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The demographic coefficients are descriptive rather than estimates of independent causal effects, because the adjustment set was designed for the focal predictors.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "we caution against interpreting the demographic coefficients\nas independent causal effects of demographics on tie formation: the adjustment set was chosen to support the interpretation of the focal predictors, and a single adjustment set is not generally appropriate for estimating every coefficient in the\nmodel.",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Peer reports used to fill in missing substance-use responses may overstate friends' use, and imputing from within the friendship network may inflate apparent similarity between friends. Excluding cases with imputed values changed the significance classification of a few estimates, mostly in School B.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "First, adolescents’ perceptions of friends’ substance use can be systematically biased, including in the direction of false consensus, in which adolescents overestimate\nfriends’ engagement in behaviors they themselves perform—and perceived friend use may diverge from friends’ own\nself-reports [34].",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "Second, because the peers providing the perceptions are themselves embedded in the friendship network, imputed values could in principle introduce or amplify apparent behavioral similarity among connected adolescents,\nif peer perceptions are shaped by reporters’ own behavior or by friendship-group norms.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "a small number of coefficients—concentrated in School B, where the excluded cases are most\nnumerous—cross conventional significance thresholds",
            "page": 7
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The AI identity measure captures strength of affirmation, not necessarily cues peers can see. The school friendship networks also leave out much of the kinship and family life where identity may matter for relationships.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "variation in the strength of identity\naffirmation (our measured construct) may not correspond to observable social cues that guide friendship decisions among\nadolescents.",
            "page": 15
          },
          {
            "text": "kinship networks and familial relationships—which operate largely outside the school-based friendship system we\nmeasured—may be the primary arena where AI identity shapes social ties [27].",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Differences among the three schools suggest contextual explanations but do not establish how community conditions or local tribal governance shape friendships.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Findings indicating differences in social networks, behaviors, and friendship formation across\nthe three schools also suggest the need to investigate how community contexts influence these factors.",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "as reservations are sovereign nations\nand have policies and laws that may differ from the rest of the state, we encourage future research to explore the role of\nlocal governance around these issues and the implications they might have on adolescent social network formation and\nrelated interventions.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The findings raise questions about the fit of existing prevention strategies but do not establish how to use the observed friendship patterns in an intervention.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "More qualitative research is needed to better understand AI adolescent networks and assess how\none can leverage existing patterns of friendship relations in interventions.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The Tribe owns the data, and the research agreement restricts public access and prohibits naming the Tribe in publications.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Data are not\npublicly available per our agreement with the\nTribe involved in this research, who owns the\ndata and has imposed the restriction.",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "Our agreement with the\nTribe and its Review Board also requires\nthat we not name the Tribe in publications.",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors combine conventional adolescent social network analysis with a culturally grounded account that treats Indigenous kinship, collectivism, and community interdependence as potential influences on how network mechanisms operate.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "We examine each attribute systematically, first summarizing evidence from non-AI adolescent populations and then articulating how the cultural context of AI-majority reservation\nschools may alter these patterns.",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "In AI reservation schools, cultural values\nemphasizing kinship, collectivism, and community interdependence provide a distinct social context that likely shapes\nthese endogenous network processes, fostering a cohesive relational fabric among adolescents [26].",
            "page": 3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The paper extends adolescent network research by using prior findings as comparative benchmarks rather than assuming they transfer unchanged to reservation schools. The authors retain broad structural mechanisms while treating their strength as dependent on the institutional setting.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "These benchmarks enable a structured\ninterpretation of whether observed patterns in AI-majority reservation schools align with or diverge from established\ntrends.",
            "page": 6
          },
          {
            "text": "Taken together, these findings suggest that while structural endogenous\nprocesses are universal, their specific parameters are calibrated by institutional context.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors challenge the adolescent-health assumption that risky behavior is a primary basis for friendship, while leaving open whether that assumption holds in settings other than the schools they studied.",
